The optimization procedure began by defining the target values and acceptable ranges for each geometrical property. These targets were established based on the intended application, process knowledge, and the available literature for the same alloy and L-DED process. The target values and limits of the desirability equations used in this work are represented in Table  6 . The target range for average peak height was defined according to the experimentally achievable values for faster and more cost-effective production. As will be seen in Sect.  3.2.2 , the average peak height ranged from 0.6 to 1.1 mm. Therefore, a target value of 0.8 mm was selected, while accepting solutions up to 1.2 mm to maximize deposition rate without compromising process stability. Deposition efficiency was maximized to improve productivity and reduce manufacturing costs by minimizing powder waste. Surface waviness was minimized because excessive waviness reduces dimensional accuracy, increases the amount of material removed during post-processing, decreases material efficiency, and may promote stress concentration [ 52 ]. Finally, dilution was optimized to ensure adequate metallurgical bonding while avoiding excessive substrate melting. Moderate dilution also helps compensate for waviness between successive layers, facilitating layer overlap and reducing the risk of lack-of-fusion defects. Based on these considerations and on recommendations in the DED literature, a target dilution range of 20–30% was adopted, consistent with the commonly recommended range of 10–30% [ 53 ,  54 ]. Table 6 Desirability criteria adopted to select the optimized L-DED single layer parameter set Desirability Functions Type A \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
