For the dilution,  S  showed a much higher impact than P, which agrees with the results shown in Fig. 16 . This trend may be observed through the surface inclination on each axis. The surfaces that represent the  HS  are close to each other, showing that  HS  does not have a high impact on dilution [ 72 ]. That space between them is an intermediary for the single layer geometrical properties of average peak height and deposition efficiency, and higher for waviness, which means that waviness is the output that is the most affected by  HS  variation. Higher  HS  generates single layers with higher waviness [ 71 ]. Average peak height has a greater impact from  S  than  P,  such as dilution, but in this case, it is inversely proportional. This occurs because by increasing  S , the amount of powder per unit length decreases [ 73 ]. Different from the other outputs, deposition efficiency tends to have a higher impact from  P  than  S . The molten pool enlarges following  P  increasing, improving powder capture. It is also possible to observe that deposition efficiency benefits from the  HS  increase [ 76 ]. The degree of the polynomial applied for each output is defined based on the representativeness of the process trends, seeking to improve R-square but avoiding overfit. In this case, the polynomials selected are of the second and third orders. Equation  8 , Eq.  9 , Eq.  10  and Eq.  11  represent each output as a function of  P ,  S  and  HS 1 : 8 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
